A||N||2||stimpy||Underhood insulation- Good idea or not?||||5||
Z||000000||stimpy||06-02-2001||08:18 PM||||I'm fixin to put on my spiffy new flat decklid this weekend (no more luggage rack, thanks Chuck!) and I am pondering putting in that relective aluminized heat shield cloth on the underside of the deck, mostly for looks but also to protect the paint from heat damage. Question is, will that effctively raise the temperature in the engine compartment? Comments?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||06-02-2001||08:54 PM||||That's a toughie. I'd say that "yes" it will raise the underhood temperatures, as it will contain the heat better. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||06-02-2001||09:13 PM||||I dont think that you will have to worry about your paint getting damaged by the heat, unless you have a really big engine and your running it long on a hot day.  Remember, all factory cars today have thier paint baked on, usually baked around 180-200 degrees.  This is also way below the flash (combust) point of paint (around 300 degrees).  There is also quite a bit of air that is flowing over the decklid as a whole, so most of heat that is absorbed through the decklid is usually carried off in the wind.<P>And yes, I think the heat sheilding will also keep some unwanted heat in, and then you might create some more engine electrical problems.  Weather-pack connectors can get very brittle after being exposed to high heat for long periods of time, same with the connectors on critical engine sensors.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
